.TH TAPE 1 "2026" "Local" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tape \- mount, detach, rewind, position, read/write, catalog, verify, query, and reset z/VM tape devices from Linux
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tape mount
[\fB\-\-volser\fR|\fB\-\-vol\fR|\fB\-v\fR \fIvolser\fR]
[\fB\-\-mode\fR|\fB\-m\fR \fIREAD\fR|\fIWRITE\fR]
[\fB\-\-read\fR|\fB\-R\fR]
[\fB\-\-write\fR|\fB\-W\fR]
[\fB\-\-dev\fR|\fB\-d\fR \fIvdev\fR]
[\fB\-\-retain\fR|\fB\-r\fR \fIdays\fR]
[\fIcommon-options\fR]
.br
.B tape detach
[\fB\-\-dev\fR|\fB\-d\fR \fIvdev\fR]
[\fB\-\-path\fR|\fB\-p\fR \fIdevice-path\fR]
.br
.B tape det
[\fB\-\-dev\fR|\fB\-d\fR \fIvdev\fR]
[\fB\-\-path\fR|\fB\-p\fR \fIdevice-path\fR]
.br
.B tape rewind
[\fB\-\-dev\fR|\fB\-d\fR \fIvdev\fR]
[\fB\-\-path\fR|\fB\-p\fR \fIdevice-path\fR]
.br
.B tape rew
[\fB\-\-dev\fR|\fB\-d\fR \fIvdev\fR]
[\fB\-\-path\fR|\fB\-p\fR \fIdevice-path\fR]
.br
.B tape fsf
[\fB\-\-dev\fR|\fB\-d\fR \fIvdev\fR]
[\fB\-\-path\fR|\fB\-p\fR \fIdevice-path\fR]
[\fB\-\-count\fR|\fB\-n\fR \fIN\fR]
.br
.B tape write
[\fB\-\-dev\fR|\fB\-d\fR \fIvdev\fR]
[\fB\-\-path\fR|\fB\-p\fR \fIdevice-path\fR]
[\fB\-\-in\fR|\fB\-i\fR \fIfile\fR]
[\fB\-\-dsn\fR|\fB\-f\fR \fIname\fR]
[\fB\-\-append\fR|\fB\-A\fR]
[\fB\-\-extra\fR|\fB\-x\fR]
.br
.B tape read
[\fB\-\-dev\fR|\fB\-d\fR \fIvdev\fR]
[\fB\-\-path\fR|\fB\-p\fR \fIdevice-path\fR]
[\fB\-\-out\fR|\fB\-o\fR \fIfile\fR]
.br
.B tape wtm
[\fB\-\-dev\fR|\fB\-d\fR \fIvdev\fR]
[\fB\-\-path\fR|\fB\-p\fR \fIdevice-path\fR]
.br
.B tape scan
[\fB\-\-dev\fR|\fB\-d\fR \fIvdev\fR]
[\fB\-\-path\fR|\fB\-p\fR \fIdevice-path\fR]
\fB\-\-dsn\fR|\fB\-f\fR \fIname\fR
[\fB\-\-count\fR|\fB\-n\fR \fIN\fR]
[\fB\-\-before\fR|\fB\-b\fR]
.br
.B tape map
[\fB\-\-dev\fR|\fB\-d\fR \fIvdev\fR]
[\fB\-\-path\fR|\fB\-p\fR \fIdevice-path\fR]
.br
.B tape verify
[\fB\-\-dev\fR|\fB\-d\fR \fIvdev\fR]
[\fB\-\-path\fR|\fB\-p\fR \fIdevice-path\fR]
[\fB\-\-checksum\fR|\fB\-C\fR \fIhex\fR]
[\fB\-\-out\fR|\fB\-o\fR \fIfile\fR]
.br
.B tape query
.br
.B tape reset
[\fB\-\-all\fR|\fB\-a\fR]
.br
.B tape \-\-help\fR|\fB\-h
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tape
is a client for the z/VM CMS Tape Proxy server. It requests virtual tape
mounts and detaches from IBM Tape Manager for z/VM on behalf of a Linux
guest running under z/VM, and manages the resulting device node ownership
and permissions so that the requesting user, and only that user, can
access the mounted tape.
.PP
The CMS Tape Proxy authenticates each request and issues the appropriate
IBM Tape Manager for z/VM command, attaching a virtual tape drive to the
Linux guest at the requested (or an automatically selected) virtual
device address. A udev-invoked helper program applies ownership and
permissions to the resulting device nodes once the driver creates them,
and reverts them to root ownership when the tape is detached.
.PP
.B tape
runs setuid root. It communicates with the CMS Tape Proxy over TCP/IP
(optionally with TLS) or over an AF_IUCV socket, and identifies the
invoking user via the real uid of the calling process, not any value
supplied by the user on the command line.
.PP
Beyond mount/detach, \fBtape\fR provides a small set of convenience
operations (\fBwrite\fR, \fBread\fR, \fBscan\fR, \fBmap\fR, \fBverify\fR)
for working with Standard Label (SL) tapes directly from Linux. These
are deliberately basic conveniences for mainframe programmers who need
to read or write labeled tape files from a Linux guest \(em not a
reimplementation of full mainframe tape-management semantics. See
\fBSTANDARD LABEL HANDLING\fR below for exactly what is, and is not,
implemented.
.PP
Because \fBtape write\fR and \fBtape read\fR move opaque binary data to
and from standard input/output when \fB\-\-in\fR/\fB\-\-out\fR are not
given, \fBtape\fR composes naturally with \fBtar\fR(1) and similar
tools to back up and restore whole directory trees as a single SL file.
See \fBBACKING UP AND RESTORING FILESYSTEMS\fR below.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B mount
Request that a tape be mounted. If \fB\-\-volser\fR (or its aliases
\fB\-\-vol\fR, \fB\-v\fR) is omitted, a SCRATCH tape is requested
instead, and the mount mode is forced to WRITE. For scratch mounts,
\fBtape\fR reads the tape's VOL1 standard label directly off the media
to determine the assigned volser; if the label cannot be read, the
mount is treated as failed \(em the device is detached automatically and
the command exits non-zero. On success, the volser, virtual device
address, mode, and (for WRITE mounts only) retention period are
reported, along with the device path to use for I/O.
.TP
.B detach\fR, \fBdet
Detach a mounted tape. If neither \fB\-\-dev\fR nor \fB\-\-path\fR is
given, the command defaults to the single tape device currently owned by
the invoking user. If the user owns more than one mounted tape, the
command lists them and requires \fB\-\-dev\fR to disambiguate. If the
user owns none, an error is reported.
.TP
.B rewind\fR, \fBrew
Rewind a mounted tape, equivalent to \fBtouch\fR on the corresponding
rewind-on-close device node, but easier to remember and type. Target
selection (\fB\-\-dev\fR, \fB\-\-path\fR, or default-to-owned-device)
follows the same rules as \fBdetach\fR.
.TP
.B fsf
Forward Space File: advance the tape past the next tape mark, using the
tape driver's own file-marking support. With \fB\-\-count\fR (alias
\fB\-n\fR), advances past that many consecutive tape marks instead of
just one. Target selection follows the same rules as \fBdetach\fR.
.TP
.B write
Write one labeled SL file to the tape: HDR1/HDR2 labels, the data
itself, EOF1/EOF2 trailer labels, then a pair of tape marks. By default,
data is read from standard input (e.g. \fBtape write < myfile.bin\fR);
with \fB\-\-in\fR (alias \fB\-i\fR), data is read from the named file
instead. Each invocation writes exactly one file; to write several
files, invoke \fBtape write\fR once per file (e.g. in a shell loop),
which also has the useful side effect of giving each file the dataset
name derived from its own filename. See \fBEXAMPLES\fR.
.IP
With \fB\-\-append\fR (alias \fB\-A\fR), the tape is first positioned
past every existing file, so this write extends the tape instead of
overwriting whatever the tape happened to be positioned at. With
\fB\-\-extra\fR (alias \fB\-x\fR), periodic progress is printed to
standard error while writing, and a sha256 checksum of the data (not
including the SL labels) is computed and shown in the completion
message; see \fBVERIFYING BACKUPS\fR.
.IP
Requires the target device to have been mounted in WRITE mode. The data
itself is written and read as opaque binary content \(em \fBtape\fR
does not interpret, convert, or care whether it is ASCII, EBCDIC, or
anything else; only the SL labels surrounding it are EBCDIC-encoded.
Target selection follows the same rules as \fBdetach\fR. To replace an
existing file (and overwrite everything physically after it on the
tape), first position the tape with
\fBtape scan \-\-dsn \fIname\fB \-\-before\fR; see \fBREPLACING A FILE\fR
below.
.TP
.B read
Read one file from the tape until a tape mark or end of data is
reached. By default, data is written to standard output (e.g.
\fBtape read > myfile.bin\fR); with \fB\-\-out\fR (alias \fB\-o\fR),
data is written to the named file instead. Like \fBwrite\fR, the data
is copied verbatim with no encoding interpretation. Target selection
follows the same rules as \fBdetach\fR.
.TP
.B wtm
Write a single tape mark with no data transfer. Requires the target
device to have been mounted in WRITE mode. Target selection follows the
same rules as \fBdetach\fR.
.TP
.B scan
Search the mounted tape's SL file labels for a file whose HDR1 dataset
name matches \fB\-\-dsn\fR (alias \fB\-f\fR), which is required for this
command. The match is case-insensitive. If the tape contains more than
one file with the same dataset name, \fB\-\-count\fR (alias \fB\-n\fR)
selects which occurrence to locate, 1-based (default: 1, the first
occurrence). If found, the tape is positioned to read that file's data
with \fBtape read\fR (or \fBtape verify\fR) \(em or, with \fB\-\-before\fR
(alias \fB\-b\fR), positioned instead to overwrite that file (and
everything physically after it) with \fBtape write\fR; see
\fBREPLACING A FILE\fR below. If the requested occurrence is not found,
the command fails (non-zero exit), reports how many occurrences (if
any) actually exist, and repositions the tape to first write position.
Target selection follows the same rules as \fBdetach\fR.
.TP
.B map
List every SL file on the mounted tape: file number, dataset name,
creation and expiration dates, and block count. Always finishes by
repositioning the tape to first write position, regardless of what it
finds. Target selection follows the same rules as \fBdetach\fR.
.TP
.B verify
Read the currently positioned file (use \fBtape scan\fR first) and
compute its sha256 checksum, over the data only \(em not the surrounding
SL labels. If \fB\-\-checksum\fR (alias \fB\-C\fR) is given, the
computed checksum is compared against it and a match or mismatch is
reported, with a non-zero exit on mismatch. If \fB\-\-checksum\fR is
omitted, the checksum is simply computed and displayed with no
comparison performed \(em useful for recovering a checksum reported
earlier by \fBtape write \-\-extra\fR and since lost, or for capturing a
reference value to compare against later. With \fB\-\-out\fR (alias
\fB\-o\fR), the data is also saved to a file while being verified. See
\fBVERIFYING BACKUPS\fR below for what each mode actually proves.
Target selection follows the same rules as \fBdetach\fR.
.TP
.B query
List currently mounted tapes. A regular user sees only tapes they
themselves have mounted. The root user sees every mounted tape on the
system, along with the owning user, mode, volser, and device path.
.TP
.B reset
Detach every tape device currently owned by the invoking user, and clear
any stale lock or owner-request files left behind by an interrupted or
inconsistent mount \(em for example, a mount that succeeded on the CMS
Tape Proxy side but whose client-side TLS handshake never completed, or
a client process terminated abnormally (\fBkill \-9\fR) before it could
perform its own cleanup.
.IP
With \fB\-\-all\fR (alias \fB\-a\fR), restricted to the root user, every
tape device in the configured range is reset regardless of owner.
Without \fB\-\-all\fR, root's \fBreset\fR affects only devices root
itself owns, the same as any other user; \fB\-\-all\fR must be given
explicitly for a system-wide sweep, so that an administrative recovery
action never silently affects other users' in-progress work by default.
.SH STANDARD LABEL HANDLING
.B tape
assumes tapes are Standard Label (SL) format. Every label record it
reads or writes \(em VOL1, HDR1, HDR2, EOF1, EOF2 \(em is EBCDIC text on
the tape (see \fBebcdic_codepage\fR under \fBFILES\fR); this applies to
all of them uniformly, not only VOL1. The actual data content moved by
\fBtape write\fR, \fBtape read\fR, and \fBtape verify\fR is never
interpreted or converted by \fBtape\fR in any way: it is copied (or
hashed) as opaque binary, whatever encoding it happens to be in.
.PP
When a virtual tape device is attached (mounted), it is already
positioned by the attach process just after the first tape mark on the
tape \(em the standard "ready to write" or "ready to read" position for
an existing volser, requiring no explicit action by \fBtape\fR itself,
for both READ and WRITE mounts.
.PP
The one exception is a SCRATCH mount: because the CMS Tape Proxy does
not necessarily report the volser it assigned, \fBtape\fR must read the
tape's VOL1 label directly to determine it, which requires an explicit
rewind; \fBtape\fR restores the standard position afterward. If the
VOL1 label cannot be read at all, the mount is treated as a failure and
the device is automatically detached.
.PP
Tapes prepared by IBM Tape Manager's scratch-pool initialization have
been observed to carry a placeholder record immediately after VOL1 \(em
labeled "HDR1" but not a real file entry (no matching HDR2, filled with
non-standard content), directly followed by a tape mark. \fBtape\fR
treats this region as opaque, read-only pre-first-file metadata and
always skips over it, both when mounting and when \fBscan\fR, \fBmap\fR,
or \fBwrite \-\-append\fR walk the tape.
.PP
.B tape write
constructs full SL records for each file: HDR1 and HDR2 (dataset name,
creation/expiration dates, block length) before the data, and EOF1 and
EOF2 (identical layout, with the actual block count now known) after
it, following each label group and the data with a tape mark. Record
format is recorded as \fBU\fR (undefined) \(em each physical block
written is a single opaque record with no internal structure, which is
the correct description of what \fBtape write\fR does. The HDR1/EOF1
"file sequence number" field is always written as 0: no reliable source
exists for this program to populate it meaningfully, and nothing in
\fBtape\fR reads this field back for any purpose \(em navigation is
always done by physically walking labels and tape marks. After the
final EOF2, two consecutive tape marks are written and then backspaced
over by one to leave the tape positioned to extend the volume with
another file; note that on some hardware/driver combinations this
backspace has been observed not to reliably collapse the two marks,
meaning more than one tape mark can end up separating consecutive
files. \fBtape scan\fR, \fBtape map\fR, and \fBtape write \-\-append\fR
all tolerate this variability when walking forward.
.PP
.B tape write \-\-append
locates the end of the tape's existing content by walking every
existing file's records explicitly (never guessing at a tapemark count
for a nonexistent "next" file, which is exactly the operation whose
mark count is hardware-dependent) and stops immediately after the last
file's EOF2, which \fBtape\fR's own reader already accepts as a valid
position from which to begin a new file's HDR1. This requires two full
passes over the tape's existing files, which is reasonable given
sequential media but means \fB\-\-append\fR's cost scales with how many
files are already on the tape.
.PP
.B tape scan
and
.B tape map
walk the SL label structure described above to locate files by dataset
name or catalog everything present. Files written by tools other than
\fBtape write\fR that do not follow this exact structure (for example,
without EOF1/EOF2) may not scan, map, or append-position correctly.
This has no effect on \fBtape read\fR or \fBtape write\fR themselves,
which operate on raw block and tape-mark boundaries and are completely
unaffected by label content either way \(em existing Linux procedures
that read or write tapes without using SL records continue to work
exactly as they always have. SL support is purely additive.
.PP
.B tape
does not write or rewrite the VOL1 volume label; volume initialization
remains the responsibility of the mainframe-side tape management system.
.SH REPLACING A FILE
.B tape scan
without \fB\-\-before\fR intentionally leaves the tape positioned to
\fIread\fR the found file \(em at the start of its DATA, past its
HDR1/HDR2 labels. Issuing \fBtape write\fR directly from that position
would leave the old HDR1/HDR2 on the tape while writing new labels and
data into the middle of the old file's DATA, corrupting the label
structure for anything that scans the tape afterward.
.PP
To replace a file (and everything physically after it on the tape) with
one or more new files, use \fB\-\-before\fR (alias \fB\-b\fR) on the
locating \fBscan\fR:
.RS
.nf
tape scan \-\-dsn MYFILE \-\-before
tape write \-\-in newfile.dat \-\-dsn MYFILE
.fi
.RE
\fB\-\-before\fR positions the tape by backing up exactly the two
physical records (HDR1 and HDR2) that were just read to identify the
match, landing precisely at the start of HDR1 regardless of any
ambiguity in surrounding tape-mark counts (see \fBSTANDARD LABEL
HANDLING\fR) \(em a fixed tape-mark backspace count was deliberately
avoided as unreliable. The subsequent \fBtape write\fR then overwrites
the old HDR1 onward with a fresh label group, data, and trailer, exactly
as it would for any other write. Additional \fBtape write\fR calls
after the first append further files normally (or use
\fBtape write \-\-append\fR to unambiguously extend past whatever the
new end of the tape now is). The old file's data, and anything that
followed it, remains physically present further along the tape but is
no longer reachable through \fBtape read\fR, \fBtape scan\fR,
\fBtape map\fR, or \fBtape verify\fR once new tape marks have been
written over that position \(em normal behavior for sequential tape
media, where nothing is erased, only superseded.
.SH BACKING UP AND RESTORING FILESYSTEMS
Because \fBtape write\fR and \fBtape read\fR move an opaque byte stream
to and from standard input/output when \fB\-\-in\fR/\fB\-\-out\fR are
not used, they compose directly with \fBtar\fR(1) (or any similar
archiver) to back up and restore a directory tree as one SL file:
.RS
.nf
tar cf \- /home | tape write \-\-dsn HOME_BACKUP \-\-extra
.fi
.RE
Restoring the whole tree, or just one file or subdirectory, both start
the same way \(em locate the file with \fBscan\fR, then let \fBtar\fR
itself decide what to extract:
.RS
.nf
tape scan \-\-dsn HOME_BACKUP
tape read | tar xf \- \-C /restore
.RE
.RS
tape scan \-\-dsn HOME_BACKUP
tape read | tar xf \- \-C /restore home/arty/one.txt
.fi
.RE
Restoring a single file or subdirectory out of a large archive does not
require reading or rewriting anything extra: \fBtar\fR reads forward
through the stream and simply discards data for members it was not
asked to extract, which is inexpensive on sequential media.
.PP
Separate backup jobs are usually best kept as separate tape files (one
\fBtape write\fR per top-level directory or generation) rather than one
combined \fBtar\fR stream, since this lets \fBtape map\fR show a real
catalog of what is on the tape and \fBtape scan \-\-dsn\fR jump directly
to the piece you want:
.RS
.nf
tar cf \- /etc  | tape write \-\-dsn ETC_20260816
tar cf \- /home | tape write \-\-dsn HOME_20260816 \-\-append
tar cf \- /var  | tape write \-\-dsn VAR_20260816  \-\-append
.fi
.RE
See \fBVERIFYING BACKUPS\fR for how to confirm a backup is intact, and
\fBREPLACING A FILE\fR for retiring an old generation in place. Note
that \fBtape\fR provides no multi-volume support (a backup exceeding
one physical tape's capacity is not automatically continued onto a
second volume) and imposes no size or format awareness beyond moving
bytes \(em compression or encryption, if wanted, should be applied as
additional pipeline stages (e.g. \fBtar cf \- /home | zstd | tape
write ...\fR) rather than expected of \fBtape\fR itself.
.SH VERIFYING BACKUPS
.B tape verify
answers two different questions depending on where the comparison
checksum comes from, and it is worth being precise about which one you
are actually asking.
.PP
Comparing against the checksum \fBtape write \-\-extra\fR reported at
write time confirms that the tape still physically contains, byte for
byte, what \fBtape\fR believed it sent to the drive. This is a genuine
round-trip check through the recording medium \(em a successful
\fBwrite\fR(2) call does not by itself guarantee correct physical
recording, and media can also degrade later \(em but it does not rule
out a bug that computes the same (wrong) checksum on both occasions,
since both values pass through the same code.
.PP
Comparing against a checksum computed independently from the original
source data (for example, running \fBtar cf \- /home | sha256sum\fR
again against the live filesystem) is a stronger, fully end-to-end
check: it confirms the tape matches the source through two entirely
separate computations of the reference value, not just a
self-comparison. Whenever practical, prefer this form for validating
that a backup actually captured what was intended.
.PP
None of this reflects a lack of trust in the tape medium itself; modern
virtual tape is frequently disk-backed and considerably more reliable
than older physical media. The failures a source-data comparison
actually catches tend to be procedural rather than hardware related
\(em an outdated build, a mistaken \fBtar\fR invocation, a truncated
pipe, or an unrelated overwrite of the wrong dataset name \(em and
these are exactly the failures medium reliability cannot rule out.
Checksumming here is best thought of as backup hygiene: cheap
insurance worth using for anything that actually matters, and freely
skippable for routine, low-stakes writes.
.PP
.B tape verify
run with no \fB\-\-checksum\fR at all performs no comparison; it simply
computes and displays the checksum of the positioned file, for example
to recover one that was lost from a prior \fBtape write \-\-extra\fR
message, or to record a reference value for later comparison.
.SH MOUNT OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-volser " " \fIvolser\fR ", " \-\-vol " " \fIvolser\fR ", " \-v " " \fIvolser\fR
Volser of the tape to mount. If omitted, a SCRATCH tape is requested.
Automatically converted to uppercase.
.TP
.BR \-\-mode " | " \-m " " \fIREAD\fR|\fIWRITE\fR
Mount mode. Case-insensitive; automatically converted to uppercase.
Defaults to READ. SCRATCH tapes are always mounted WRITE; specifying
\fB\-\-mode READ\fR (or \fB\-\-read\fR/\fB\-R\fR) together with a
SCRATCH mount (i.e. no \fB\-\-volser\fR) is an error.
.TP
.B \-\-read\fR, \fB\-R
Alias for \fB\-\-mode READ\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-write\fR, \fB\-W
Alias for \fB\-\-mode WRITE\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-dev " | " \-d " " \fIvdev\fR
Virtual device address to use, in any of the forms \fI181\fR,
\fI0181\fR, \fI0x181\fR, or \fI0.0.0181\fR. If omitted, the first free
address in the configured range (default \fI0180\fR\-\fI018F\fR) is
selected and atomically reserved before the mount request is sent.
.TP
.BR \-\-retain " | " \-r " " \fIdays\fR
Retention period in days. Defaults to 7, or the value of
\fBretention_default\fR in the configuration file if set. Rejected if it
exceeds \fBretention_max\fR (see \fBFILES\fR), when configured. Only
meaningful for WRITE mounts; not shown in the mount success message for
READ mounts.
.SH DETACH / REWIND / FSF / WRITE / READ / WTM / SCAN / MAP / VERIFY OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-dev " | " \-d " " \fIvdev\fR
Virtual device address, in any of the forms accepted by \fB\-\-dev\fR
under \fBmount\fR. If omitted, defaults to the tape currently owned by
the invoking user (see \fBCOMMANDS\fR above).
.TP
.BR \-\-path " | " \-p " " \fIdevice-path\fR
Device path (e.g. \fI/dev/ntibm0\fR) identifying the target tape, as an
alternative to \fB\-\-dev\fR.
.SH WRITE OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-in " | " \-i " " \fIfile\fR
Read data to write from this file instead of standard input. Accepts a
single filename; if the shell expands a glob (e.g. \fB*.c\fR) into this
option, only the first match is used, since \fB\-\-in\fR takes exactly
one argument. To write several files, invoke \fBtape write\fR once per
file instead of relying on shell expansion; see \fBEXAMPLES\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-dsn " | " \-f " " \fIname\fR
HDR1 dataset name, up to 17 characters. If omitted: when \fB\-\-in\fR is
given, defaults to that file's name (basename only, uppercased,
truncated to 17 characters); otherwise defaults to the tape's volser.
.TP
.B \-\-append\fR, \fB\-A
Position the tape past every existing file before writing, so this
call extends the tape with a new file rather than overwriting whatever
the tape is currently positioned at. Requires two full passes over the
existing files on the tape (see \fBSTANDARD LABEL HANDLING\fR).
.TP
.B \-\-extra\fR, \fB\-x
Print periodic progress to standard error while writing, and compute a
sha256 checksum of the data (excluding SL labels), shown in the
completion message. See \fBVERIFYING BACKUPS\fR.
.SH READ OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-out " | " \-o " " \fIfile\fR
Write the file's data to this file instead of standard output.
.SH SCAN OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-dsn " | " \-f " " \fIname\fR
Dataset name to search for; required for this command. Matched
case-insensitively against each file's HDR1 dataset name.
.TP
.BR \-\-count " | " \-n " " \fIN\fR
Which occurrence of the dataset name to locate, 1-based. Defaults to 1
(the first occurrence). Useful when \fBtape map\fR shows multiple files
sharing the same dataset name.
.TP
.B \-\-before\fR, \fB\-b
Position the tape to overwrite the found file (and everything
physically after it) with \fBtape write\fR, instead of positioning to
read it. See \fBREPLACING A FILE\fR.
.SH VERIFY OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-checksum " | " \-C " " \fIhex\fR
Expected sha256 checksum to compare against. Optional: if omitted, the
checksum of the positioned file is computed and displayed instead of
compared. See \fBVERIFYING BACKUPS\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-out " | " \-o " " \fIfile\fR
Also save the file's data to this file while computing its checksum.
.SH FSF OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-count " | " \-n " " \fIN\fR
Number of consecutive tape marks to forward space past. Defaults to 1.
Note that \fB\-\-count\fR/\fB\-n\fR means something different on
\fBscan\fR (an occurrence index) than on \fBfsf\fR (a tape mark count);
context always disambiguates which is meant.
.SH RESET OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-all\fR, \fB\-a
Restricted to the root user. Resets every tape device in the configured
range system-wide, rather than only devices owned by the invoking user.
Using this option as a non-root user is an error.
.SH TRANSPORT OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-host " " \fIhost\fR
CMS Tape Proxy hostname or IP address (TCP/IP mode). Defaults to
\fIlocalhost\fR, or the value of \fBhost\fR in the configuration file.
.TP
.BR \-\-port " " \fIport\fR
CMS Tape Proxy TCP port (TCP/IP mode). Defaults to \fI4443\fR, or the
value of \fBport\fR in the configuration file.
.TP
.B \-\-noTLS
Disable TLS and use plaintext TCP/IP. Equivalent to setting
\fBtls=no\fR in the configuration file. A \fB\-\-noTLS\fR given on the
command line always takes precedence over the configuration file's
\fBtls=\fR setting.
.TP
.BR \-\-ca\-file " " \fIpath\fR
CA bundle used to verify the CMS Tape Proxy's TLS certificate, including
hostname verification against \fB\-\-host\fR. If not set (and not set
via \fBca_file\fR in the configuration file), TLS is still used when
enabled, but the server's certificate is \fBnot\fR verified: the
connection is encrypted but not authenticated, and a warning is printed
on every TLS mount. Strongly recommended whenever TLS is enabled.
.TP
.BR \-\-key\-file " " \fIpath\fR
Client private key, for mutual TLS. Must be set together with
\fB\-\-certificate\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-certificate " " \fIpath\fR
Client certificate, for mutual TLS. Must be set together with
\fB\-\-key\-file\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-iucv
Use an AF_IUCV socket instead of TCP/IP. The connection is made in
blocking mode (see \fBAF_IUCV NOTES\fR); \fB\-\-timeout\fR/\fB\-t\fR is
accepted but has no practical effect on the connect step itself, since
IUCV connection failures are reflected immediately by CP. Requires
\fB\-\-iucv\-userid\fR (or \fBiucv_userid\fR in the configuration file).
.TP
.BR \-\-iucv\-userid " " \fIVMUSERID\fR
Target z/VM guest userid of the CMS Tape Proxy service machine. Required
when \fB\-\-iucv\fR is used.
.TP
.BR \-\-iucv\-name " " \fIname\fR
Target IUCV application name of the CMS Tape Proxy. Defaults to
\fITAPESRV\fR, or the value of \fBiucv_name\fR in the configuration
file.
.SH COMMON OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-config " | " \-c " " \fIpath\fR
Path to an alternate configuration file. See \fBFILES\fR below for the
default search order.
.TP
.BR \-\-password " | " \-P " " \fIpass\fR
Password presented to the CMS Tape Proxy, if required by its
authentication scheme. May also be set via \fBpassword\fR in the
configuration file. Not applicable when \fB\-\-iucv\fR is used, since
IUCV authorization is enforced by z/VM's CP directory rather than a
shared secret (see \fBSECURITY\fR).
.TP
.BR \-\-timeout " | " \-t " " \fIseconds\fR
Timeout, in seconds, to wait for the mounted tape's device node to
appear and become owned by the invoking user before reporting failure.
Defaults to 30, or the value of \fBtimeout\fR in the configuration file.
Does not affect the AF_IUCV connect step itself; see \fB\-\-iucv\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-debug\fR, \fB\-D
Print diagnostic detail to standard error.
.TP
.B \-\-help\fR, \fB\-h
Display usage and exit.
.PP
Note: \fB\-\-host\fR, \fB\-\-port\fR, \fB\-\-noTLS\fR, \fB\-\-ca\-file\fR,
\fB\-\-key\-file\fR, \fB\-\-certificate\fR, \fB\-\-iucv\fR,
\fB\-\-iucv\-userid\fR, and \fB\-\-iucv\-name\fR have no short-option
form, since these are typically set once via the configuration file
rather than supplied interactively on each invocation.
.SH AF_IUCV NOTES
When \fB\-\-iucv\fR is used, the connection is established with a
blocking \fBconnect\fR(2) call rather than the non-blocking-plus-
\fBselect\fR(2) pattern used for TCP/IP. This was a deliberate choice
after testing: non-blocking \fBconnect\fR(2) on \fBAF_IUCV\fR sockets
was not reliable, and IUCV connection failures (for example, an
unauthorized target userid) are reported immediately by z/VM's CP
rather than requiring an asynchronous wait, so there is little
practical benefit to a non-blocking approach here in the first place.
\fB\-\-timeout\fR/\fB\-t\fR is still accepted for consistency with the
TCP/IP path but has no effect on how long the IUCV connect step itself
can take.
.PP
On \fBAF_IUCV\fR sockets, a non-blocking \fBconnect\fR(2) that has not
yet completed has been observed to report \fBEAGAIN\fR/\fBEWOULDBLOCK\fR
(errno 11) rather than the \fBEINPROGRESS\fR that most other socket
families use for the same situation; both are treated equivalently
wherever this program still checks for that condition, for anyone
extending this code in the future.
.SH GROUP MEMBERSHIP REQUIREMENT
Only members of the Linux group \fBtapes\fR may mount tapes, whether by
volser or as a scratch request. This is enforced both by filesystem
permissions on the \fBtape\fR binary itself (owned \fBroot:tapes\fR,
mode \fI4750\fR, so only \fBtapes\fR group members can execute it at
all) and, redundantly, by \fBtape mount\fR checking group membership
directly before contacting the CMS Tape Proxy.
.PP
\fBdetach\fR, \fBrewind\fR, \fBfsf\fR, \fBwrite\fR, \fBread\fR,
\fBwtm\fR, \fBscan\fR, \fBmap\fR, \fBverify\fR, \fBquery\fR, and
\fBreset\fR additionally require: \fBdetach\fR, \fBrewind\fR, \fBfsf\fR,
\fBwrite\fR, \fBread\fR, \fBwtm\fR, \fBscan\fR, \fBmap\fR, and
\fBverify\fR require the invoking user to \fBown\fR the target device
node (established by device ownership applied at mount time);
\fBwrite\fR and \fBwtm\fR further require the device to have been
mounted in WRITE mode; \fBquery\fR restricts a non-root caller's
results to tapes that user has mounted; and a non-root user's
\fBreset\fR affects only devices that user itself owns.
.PP
Administrators should add authorized users to the \fBtapes\fR group,
e.g.:
.RS
.nf
usermod \-aG tapes \fIusername\fR
.fi
.RE
.SH DEVICE OWNERSHIP AND PERMISSIONS
When a tape is mounted, the resulting device node is owned by the
invoking user and group \fBroot\fR, with permissions set according to
mount mode:
.TP
.B WRITE
mode \fI0600\fR (read/write by owner only)
.TP
.B READ
mode \fI0400\fR (read-only by owner only)
.PP
On detach (including via \fBreset\fR, or automatically if a scratch
mount's VOL1 label cannot be read), ownership reverts to
\fBroot:root\fR with permissions \fI0000\fR. Ownership and permission
changes are applied by a udev-invoked helper program, not by \fBtape\fR
itself, based on device attach/remove events.
.PP
The \fBtape\fR binary itself is owned \fBroot:tapes\fR, mode \fI4750\fR
(setuid root, group-executable only by \fBtapes\fR members).
.PP
The channel-attached tape driver additionally exposes a block-device
node (\fI/dev/btibm\fIn\fR) alongside the character devices
(\fI/dev/ntibm\fIn\fR, \fI/dev/rtibm\fIn\fR) used throughout this
program; \fBtape\fR does not manage ownership of \fI/dev/btibm\fIn\fR
and has no facility for mounting a filesystem from tape. This is a
known, deliberately out-of-scope item, not an oversight.
.SH RECOVERING FROM INCONSISTENT STATE
Under normal operation, \fBtape mount\fR and \fBtape detach\fR keep the
following three things synchronized: the actual attached device, a
per-device lock file under \fI/run/lock/tape\fR used to prevent two
simultaneous mount requests from racing for the same virtual device
address, and a per-device owner-request file under \fI/run/tape\fR that
records who should own the resulting device node.
.PP
These can become inconsistent if a mount is interrupted after the CMS
Tape Proxy has confirmed the mount but before the client finishes
waiting for the device, or if the \fBtape\fR process is killed
abnormally before it can run its own cleanup, or if \fBvmcp det\fR is
issued directly against a device outside of \fBtape detach\fR. In such
cases a lock or owner-request file may be left behind, owned by root,
which an unprivileged user cannot remove directly.
.PP
None of this affects data already written to tape: each \fBtape write\fR
call is fully self-contained on the media (see \fBSTANDARD LABEL
HANDLING\fR), so losing the tracking file has no effect on files already
written.
.PP
Run
.B tape reset
to detach any devices still owned by the invoking user and remove any
stale lock or owner-request files associated with them. See the
\fBreset\fR entry under \fBCOMMANDS\fR for details, including the
distinct behavior of \fB\-\-all\fR for the root user.
.SH SECURITY
.B tape
runs setuid root because it must issue \fBchccwdev\fR(8) and
\fBvmcp\fR(1) commands, and must create files under \fI/run/lock/tape\fR
and \fI/run/tape\fR that a udev-invoked helper reads to determine device
ownership. All external commands are invoked directly via
\fBfork\fR(2)/\fBexecvp\fR(2) rather than through a shell, since setuid
programs that shell out are subject to the shell's own privilege-drop
behavior when real and effective uid differ.
.PP
Disabling TLS (via \fB\-\-noTLS\fR or \fBtls=no\fR) removes
transport-layer confidentiality and integrity protection for TCP/IP
connections to the CMS Tape Proxy. Prefer \fB\-\-iucv\fR where
available, since AF_IUCV transport is authenticated at the z/VM CP
directory level regardless of any TLS setting.
.PP
When TLS is enabled without \fBca_file\fR configured, the connection is
encrypted but the server's identity is not verified. Configure
\fBca_file\fR (and, for mutual TLS, \fBcertificate\fR and \fBkey_file\fR
together) whenever TLS is used.
.PP
When \fB\-\-iucv\fR is used, the identity of the calling Linux guest is
authenticated by z/VM's CP directory, rather than by a shared secret
passed over the wire. The identity of the invoking Linux user is
separately established by this program's setuid-root status and the
real uid of the calling process.
.PP
\fBtape\fR does not write the VOL1 volume label; volume initialization
remains the responsibility of the mainframe-side tape management system.
.PP
\fBtape reset\fR can only detach devices the invoking user already
owns, unless \fB\-\-all\fR is used, which is restricted to root.
.PP
Checksums computed by \fB\-\-extra\fR and \fBtape verify\fR are sha256
digests of file data only, held in memory for the duration of the
command and never persisted to disk or to the tape itself; if you want
to keep one, capture it from the command's output.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/tape.conf
System-wide configuration file.
.TP
.I ~/.tape.conf
Per-user configuration file.
.TP
.I ./tape.conf
Configuration file in the current directory.
.PP
Configuration files are searched in the order: the path given by
\fB\-\-config\fR, \fI./tape.conf\fR, \fI~/.tape.conf\fR, then
\fI/etc/tape.conf\fR. The first file found is used; settings are not
merged across files.
.PP
Recognized configuration keys:
.RS
.nf
host               = \fIhostname\fR
port               = \fIport\fR
password           = \fIpassword\fR
timeout            = \fIseconds\fR      (default 30)
vdev_min           = \fIhex vdev\fR    (e.g. 180)
vdev_max           = \fIhex vdev\fR    (e.g. 18F)
tls                = yes|no           (default yes; overridden by \-\-noTLS)
ca_file            = \fIpath\fR         (CA bundle to verify server cert)
key_file           = \fIpath\fR         (client key, mutual TLS)
certificate        = \fIpath\fR         (client cert, mutual TLS)
retention_default  = \fIdays\fR         (default 7)
retention_max      = \fIdays\fR         (no default limit)
ebcdic_codepage    = \fIcodepage\fR     (default IBM037; applies to ALL
                                        SL label records - VOL1, HDR1,
                                        HDR2, EOF1, EOF2 - not just VOL1)
iucv               = yes|no
iucv_userid        = \fIVMUSERID\fR
iucv_name          = \fIname\fR
.fi
.RE
.PP
Values may be quoted with single or double quotes; blank lines and
lines beginning with \fB#\fR are ignored. The \fBtls\fR, \fBca_file\fR,
\fBkey_file\fR, and \fBcertificate\fR keys are ignored when \fBiucv =
yes\fR. \fBcertificate\fR and \fBkey_file\fR must both be set, or
neither, for mutual TLS to take effect.
.TP
.I /run/lock/tape/\fIvdev\fR.lock
Transient lock file used to atomically reserve a virtual device address
during mount processing.
.TP
.I /run/tape/\fIvdev\fR.owner
Transient file recording the owning uid, mode, volser, and retention
for a mounted device.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B tape
exits 0 on success and a non-zero value on any error, including
permission denial, an unavailable or already-reserved device, a failed
or rejected request from the CMS Tape Proxy, an unreadable VOL1 label on
a scratch mount, a dataset (or requested occurrence) not found by
\fBscan\fR, a checksum mismatch reported by \fBverify\fR, or a timeout
waiting for the device to become available.
.SH EXAMPLES
Mount a specific volser for writing on a specific device, with a
30-day retention:
.RS
.nf
tape mount \-\-volser ABC123 \-\-mode WRITE \-\-dev 181 \-\-retain 30
tape mount \-v ABC123 \-W \-d 181 \-r 30
.fi
.RE
Mount a volser for reading, letting the system pick a free device:
.RS
.nf
tape mount \-\-vol ABC123
.fi
.RE
Mount a scratch tape:
.RS
.nf
tape mount
.fi
.RE
Write a file to tape (dsn defaults to the file's name, uppercased):
.RS
.nf
tape write \-\-in Makefile
tape write \-i Makefile
.fi
.RE
Write a file to tape with an explicit dataset name:
.RS
.nf
tape write \-i myfile.bin \-f PAYROLL
.fi
.RE
Write an entire directory of files to tape as one file per name, each
dsn taken from its own filename:
.RS
.nf
for f in *.c; do tape write \-\-in "$f"; done
.fi
.RE
Read a tape file back to a local file:
.RS
.nf
tape read \-\-out myfile.bin
tape read \-o myfile.bin
.fi
.RE
Write a tape mark with no data (e.g. to close out a multi-file volume):
.RS
.nf
tape wtm
.fi
.RE
Skip forward past the next tape mark:
.RS
.nf
tape fsf
.fi
.RE
Search for a file by dataset name and read it:
.RS
.nf
tape scan \-\-dsn payroll
tape read \-o payroll.dat
.fi
.RE
Locate the third file named PAYROLL and read it:
.RS
.nf
tape scan \-f payroll \-n 3
tape read \-o payroll3.dat
.fi
.RE
Replace an existing file (and everything after it) with a new one:
.RS
.nf
tape scan \-\-dsn payroll \-\-before
tape write \-i newpayroll.dat \-f PAYROLL
.fi
.RE
List every file on the mounted tape:
.RS
.nf
tape map
.fi
.RE
Back up a directory tree, capturing a checksum:
.RS
.nf
tar cf \- /home | tape write \-\-dsn HOME_BACKUP \-\-extra
.fi
.RE
Restore it in full, or just one file or subdirectory:
.RS
.nf
tape scan \-\-dsn HOME_BACKUP
tape read | tar xf \- \-C /restore
tape scan \-\-dsn HOME_BACKUP
tape read | tar xf \- \-C /restore home/arty/one.txt
.fi
.RE
Add another backup after the first, without disturbing it:
.RS
.nf
tar cf \- /var | tape write \-\-append \-\-dsn VAR_BACKUP
.fi
.RE
Confirm the tape still matches what was written:
.RS
.nf
tape scan \-\-dsn HOME_BACKUP
tape verify \-C \fI<checksum-from-write>\fR
.fi
.RE
Confirm the tape matches the source data right now (strongest check):
.RS
.nf
tape scan \-\-dsn HOME_BACKUP
tape verify \-C "$(tar cf \- /home | sha256sum | cut \-d' ' \-f1)"
.fi
.RE
Recover a checksum that was lost, with no comparison:
.RS
.nf
tape scan \-\-dsn HOME_BACKUP
tape verify
.fi
.RE
Detach the tape currently mounted by the caller:
.RS
.nf
tape detach
.fi
.RE
Detach a specific device:
.RS
.nf
tape det \-\-dev 181
.fi
.RE
Rewind the caller's mounted tape:
.RS
.nf
tape rewind
.fi
.RE
Mount over AF_IUCV instead of TCP/IP:
.RS
.nf
tape mount \-\-iucv \-\-iucv\-userid TAPESRV1 \-\-vol ABC123
.fi
.RE
List the caller's mounted tapes:
.RS
.nf
tape query
.fi
.RE
Clear stuck reservations after an interrupted mount:
.RS
.nf
tape reset
.fi
.RE
Root: reset every user's tapes system-wide:
.RS
.nf
tape reset \-\-all
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR chccwdev (8),
.BR vmcp (1),
.BR mt (1),
.BR tar (1),
.BR sha256sum (1),
.BR udev (7)
